This file will not explain Pebble. Please view https://pebblevm.org/ for this.

Building from source

	CPebble uses its own build infa. This can be found in the build/ directory
	relative to the root of this repo. This tool exposes items to set specific
	values for comptime-tuning

		build		Type of build to use; values are debug for debugging
				builds which include debug opcodes and keep in info
				useful for debugging, default as the default / release
				build, fast as a faster version of release, which is
				NOT portable across microarchitectures (do NOT run it
				on any machine besides the host compiling), small, which
				sacrifices performance for binary size, xray, which
				includes clang-specific flags for LLVM X-ray debugging, 
				and bad, which omits compiler and linker optimization.
				The default option is default.

		platform	Platform backend to se for the build; backends include
				freestand for pure c (which has no entry code and WILL
				NOT compile by itself), c, which assumes a standard C99
				hosted Libc, posix, which assumes a POSIX-1.2008
				compliant system, and openbsd, which assumes an OpenBSD
				Libc and kernel. The puredarwin platform is depreciated.
				Platform auto-detection is supported and this will
				default to whatever it detects.

		cflags		Option to append extra flags to pass to the C compiler
				after the default ones are applied.

		ldflags		Option to append extra flags to pass to the linker
				after the default ones are applied.

		terminal	Sets the terminal backend for terminal manipulation.
				Default is ansi, for an ANSI compliant terminal. Not
				required.

		precc		The command to run before the C compiler, eg.
				(precc) (cc) ... This is not required.

		analyzer	Alias of precc

		proc		Enable process-manipulation libraries if this is
				'yes'. Not required. Defaults to unset.

		fs		Enable filesystem management libraries if this is
				'yes'. Not required. Defaults to unset.

		graphics	Graphics backend. Deprecated. Defaults to unset. Not
				required.

		program		The output binary. Default is 'bin/cpebble'

		cc		The C compiler to use. Default is cc.

	These can be selected by setting (name)=(value) or (name)='(value)' as any
	of the command arguments.

	The build system uses basic threading for source compiling. If you are on
	a UNIX-like platform, please make sure to link in pthread when compiling
	the build system. On non-UNIX systems, multithreading is not used in
	compiling and this is not needed.

	The New Rock compiler requires the helper utility found at newrock/embed.c.
	This is used to generate the header file from the New Rock standard library
	source code. This tool takes two arguments, the first being the path to the
	file to generate a header from, and the second being the name to generate the
	variable prefix. Set the first to newrock/rockc/stdlib.newrock and the second
	to stdlib. Pipe the output of this program into newrock/rockc/stdlib.h.

	The rockpkg package manager can be built simply by compiling the source code.
	It should be noted this tool requires the libcurl library to be included.

	The New Rock package manager expects utility rockc to point to a New Rock
	compiler.

	The New Rock compiler expects the utility pblvm to point to a Pebble interpreter,
	such as CPebble. It addresses it by /usr/bin/env pblvm

	Please make sure both of these are present in your $PATH.


	An example building the full thing from source, assuming a BSD system:

		mkdir bin
		cc -o bin/make build/*.c -pthread
		./bin/make fs=yes proc=yes
		cc -o bin/embed newrock/embed.c
		./bin/embed newrock/rockc/stdlib.newrock stdlib \
			>newrock/rockc/stdlib.h
		cc -o bin/rockc newrock/rockc/*.c
		cc -o bin/rockpkg newrock/rockpkg/*.c -I/usr/local/include \
			-L/usr/local/lib -lcurl
		
	And now you have a Pebble interpreter at bin/cpebble, a New Rock
	compiler at bin/rockc, and the New Rock package manager at
	bin/rockpkg.

	Please make sure to run the torture test suite against your build
	of CPebble after compiling with

		./bin/cpebble tools/tests/torture.pebble

	This will simply print 'PASS' if the test passed, or 'FAIL' with
	the expected and the given results if the test failed.

Usage

	The interpreter accepts one argument, being the path to the file
	containing instructions encoded under the Pebble textual bytecode
	format (.pebble). More options may be supported. To view these
	extras, run the interpreter with the first argument of 'help' or
	'--help'. This will print usage info onto stdout.

	The New Rock compiler accepts two arguments, the first being the
	path to the New Rock (.newrock) source code file to compile and
	the second being the directory to search for the New Rock include
	keyword. It will write the program onto stdout in the Pebble
	textual bytecode format, adding the shebang '#!/usr/bin/env pblvm'
	to the top. If there is an error when compiling, nothing will
	be written to stdout, and the error message will be written to
	stderr.

	The rockpkg package manager accepts the first command as the
	operation to perform and the second as the path to the New Rock
	Package file (.rockpkg) in reference. This will set up the user
	directories, read the file and perform the operation requested.
	Supported operations include 'install' to install a package and
	'remove' to delete a package.

	Please make sure you have a copy of the New Rock compiler in
	your $PATH variable under the name 'rockc' and a copy of The
	Pebble Virtual Machine under the name 'pblvm', as the standard
	suggests.

	Please also make sure your $HOME is writable by your user and
	the path '$HOME/.rock/bin/' is in your $PATH.

Binaries

	Some releases may be able to provide pre-built binaries. If this is
	the case, the bin/ directory will exist, and will be populated
	with the pre-built binaries in reference. There will be subdirs
	inside of the directory, grouped by the operating system then
	the ISA the binary is made for, eg. 'bin/openbsd/amd64/'. A
	readme file will also be present describing the machine and
	build arguments used to build the binaries in reference.

NOTICE

	Code generated by a large language model is not allowed. The
	true source of this code cannot be easily verified, and it
	therefore cannot be safely licensed by the author in all
	cases.

	This rule applies the canonical upstream repo as well as all
	mirrors of it maintained by The Nuovi Orizzonti Company.

	Please do not attempt to submit code generated by a large
	language model.
